<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Nazca</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 750 CE (approx.) · capital Cahuachi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A culture of Peru's southern desert famed for the Nazca Lines, giant figures and lines drawn on the desert floor, and for its brightly painted pottery.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Nazca culture grew out of the late <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/paracas">Paracas</a> tradition around 100 BCE in the Río Grande de Nazca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ica-river">Ica</a> valleys of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In its early phase the adobe ceremonial centre of Cahuachi, with dozens of mounds, served as a pilgrimage site for scattered farming communities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nazca appears to have been a group of chiefdoms linked by shared ritual rather than a centralised state. Its people created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines">Nazca Lines</a>, hundreds of straight lines and figures of a monkey, hummingbird and spider scraped into the pampa, likely connected to water and fertility rites. They built underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> called puquios to tap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>. Their polychrome pottery used up to a dozen slip colours, and trophy heads feature prominently in their art.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cahuachi lost importance by about 400 CE, and late Nazca society suffered from droughts and conflict. From around 650 CE the region came under the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wari-empire">Wari Empire</a>, and the Nazca style faded. The Lines became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1994.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nazca.jpg" alt="Nazca" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Murray Foubister · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaza_de_Armas,_Nazca_(8443289015).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 750 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cahuachi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Unknown local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Peru</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nasca, Nazca culture</dd></dl></aside></div>
